---
import codexSkillsImage from "@/assets/my-current-agentic-software-development-workflow/codex-skills-used.png";
import ArticleRail from "@/components/ArticleRail.astro";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const sections = [
  { id: "the-workflow-is-the-point", label: "The workflow is the point" },
  { id: "planning-needs-my-attention", label: "Planning needs my attention" },
  { id: "turn-the-conversation-into-work", label: "Turn the conversation into work" },
  { id: "one-ticket-one-session-one-worktree", label: "One ticket, one session, one worktree" },
  { id: "the-small-task-shortcut", label: "The small-task shortcut" },
  { id: "ui-work-needs-two-loops", label: "UI work needs two loops" },
  { id: "parallel-work-follows-the-tickets", label: "Parallel work follows the tickets" },
  { id: "why-i-do-not-read-every-diff", label: "Why I do not read every diff" },
  { id: "the-tools-can-change", label: "The tools can change" },
  { id: "this-is-a-snapshot", label: "This is a snapshot" },
  { id: "references", label: "References" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="My current agentic software development workflow"
  description="How I plan, split, implement, test, and merge software with AI coding agents."
>
  <article class="page-stack writing-article">
    <ArticleRail sections={sections} />

    <header class="article-header">
      <a class="back-link" href="/writing">Writing</a>
      <div class="article-heading">
        <p class="article-kicker">Sep 2026</p>
        <h1>My current agentic software development workflow</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">
          Where I spend my attention, what I hand to agents, and why I rarely read every line they
          write
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <section aria-labelledby="the-workflow-is-the-point">
        <h2 id="the-workflow-is-the-point">The workflow is the point</h2>
        <p>
          Between August 6 and September 4, Codex recorded 1,490 skill invocations while I worked.
          That sounds like a lot of automation. I still refuse to automate the part that matters
          most. Planning.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number is not a quality score. It only shows that this is how I work every day. I am
          not describing a workflow I tried for a weekend.
        </p>

        <figure class="article-figure analytics-figure">
          <img
            src={codexSkillsImage.src}
            alt="Codex analytics chart showing 1,490 skill invocations between August 6 and September 4, including Implement, Code Review, Grill Me, Browser, Grill With Docs, Shadcn, Research, and Unslop."
            width={codexSkillsImage.width}
            height={codexSkillsImage.height}
            loading="eager"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>
            My Codex skill usage from August 6 to September 4. Volume is evidence of use, not proof
            that the process is correct.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>The full path looks like this:</p>
        <figure class="workflow-figure" aria-labelledby="workflow-caption">
          <ol class="workflow-flow">
            <li><span>01</span>Grill</li>
            <li><span>02</span>Spec</li>
            <li><span>03</span>Tickets</li>
            <li><span>04</span>Implement</li>
            <li><span>05</span>Verify</li>
            <li><span>06</span>Merge</li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="workflow-caption">
            I stay present for the first three steps. Fresh agent sessions take over once the work
            is settled.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="planning-needs-my-attention">
        <h2 id="planning-needs-my-attention">Planning needs my attention</h2>
        <p>
          For a new app or feature, I start with Matt Pocock's
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills">engineering skills</a>.<sup>
            <a href="#ref-1">1</a>
          </sup>
          I use
          <code>grill-me</code> when the idea is not tied to an existing codebase. Inside a repo, I
          use
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/grill-with-docs.md">
            <code>grill-with-docs</code>
          </a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is the human-in-the-loop phase. The agent asks one question at a time, recommends an
          answer, and keeps following the decision tree until we agree on what to build. I give this
          phase my full attention. I cannot go AFK while the agent is asking me what the product
          should do.
        </p>
        <p>
          The grilling catches unclear behavior, awkward edge cases, and disagreements in language
          before they turn into code. It is slower than sending one large implementation prompt.
          That is fine. The time I spend here removes decisions from the expensive part of the
          process.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="turn-the-conversation-into-work">
        <h2 id="turn-the-conversation-into-work">Turn the conversation into work</h2>
        <p>
          Once we share an understanding, I stay in the same session and invoke
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/to-spec.md">
            <code>to-spec</code>
          </a>. The back-and-forth is valuable context. Starting a fresh chat here would throw away
          the reasons behind the decisions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The skill turns that conversation into a spec with the problem, user stories,
          implementation decisions, testing decisions, and explicit exclusions. I currently
          publish the spec to GitHub Issues. Linear would work too.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next I use
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/to-tickets.md">
            <code>to-tickets</code>
          </a>. It breaks the spec into small vertical slices. Each ticket delivers behavior across
          the layers it touches, has its own acceptance criteria, and names the other tickets that
          block it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ticket size matters because long sessions get worse as irrelevant history and tool output
          accumulate. Dex Horthy calls the degraded part of a crowded context window the
          <a href="https://ai.engineer/talks/context-engineering-for-complex-codebases">
            "dumb zone"
          </a>.<sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup> I do not treat it as a fixed percentage. I treat it
          as a reason to give a fresh agent one bounded job.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="one-ticket-one-session-one-worktree">
        <h2 id="one-ticket-one-session-one-worktree">One ticket, one session, one worktree</h2>
        <p>
          My implementation unit is strict: one ticket, one session, one Git worktree, one
          implementation. I point the session at the ticket and invoke
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/implement.md">
            <code>implement</code>
          </a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          By then the product decisions are already made. The agent can focus on execution. The
          implementation skill uses TDD at the agreed test seams, runs type checks as it works, runs
          the full test suite at the end, and calls a separate code-review skill before it commits.
          For browser behavior, I also expect Playwright coverage where it makes sense.
        </p>
        <p>
          I sometimes describe this as testing a ticket to 100 percent. I do not mean 100 percent
          code coverage or zero chance of a defect. I mean every acceptance criterion in the ticket
          must have evidence before the implementation is done.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="the-small-task-shortcut">
        <h2 id="the-small-task-shortcut">The small-task shortcut</h2>
        <p>
          Not every change needs a spec and a ticket breakdown. Adding a functional option to a menu
          or fixing a contained bug can go straight to <code>implement</code> in the current session.
        </p>
        <p>
          I still use <code>grill-me</code> when a small task hides uncertain behavior or edge cases.
          The shortcut depends on how many decisions remain, not how many lines of code I expect.
          Once the behavior is clear, I skip <code>to-spec</code> and <code>to-tickets</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="ui-work-needs-two-loops">
        <h2 id="ui-work-needs-two-loops">UI work needs two loops</h2>
        <p>
          For UI changes, I attach a reference image and keep iterating until the result matches what
          I had in mind. This is one place where my eyes remain part of the test. A browser test can
          prove that a menu opens, a route changes, or state persists. It cannot decide whether the
          page looks right.
        </p>
        <p>
          I run both loops. Visual comparison handles appearance. Playwright handles behavior. A
          screenshot does not replace interaction tests, and passing interaction tests do not make a
          weak visual result acceptable.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="parallel-work-follows-the-tickets">
        <h2 id="parallel-work-follows-the-tickets">Parallel work follows the tickets</h2>
        <p>
          The blocking links from <code>to-tickets</code> decide what can run at the same time. If
          three tickets have no unfinished blockers, I can open three sessions. Each session gets
          its own worktree and its own ticket. I start the implementations, go AFK, and wait for the
          Codex notifications.
        </p>
        <p>
          Parallel work occasionally produces merge conflicts. When that happens, I use the
          <a
            href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/resolving-merge-conflicts.md"
          >
            <code>resolving-merge-conflicts</code>
          </a>
          skill. It reads the intent behind both changes, resolves the hunks, and runs the project's
          checks again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once a branch is ready, the official GitHub plugin's
          <a href="https://github.com/openai/plugins/blob/main/plugins/github/skills/yeet/SKILL.md">
            <code>github:yeet</code>
          </a>
          skill checks the change, commits it, pushes the branch, and opens a draft PR.<sup>
            <a href="#ref-3">3</a>
          </sup>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="why-i-do-not-read-every-diff">
        <h2 id="why-i-do-not-read-every-diff">Why I do not read every diff</h2>
        <p>
          This still feels strange to say, but I rarely review the generated code line by line. I
          open the app, exercise the feature, and confirm that it behaves as intended. When that
          check passes, I approve the PR and merge it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am not trusting a single model response. I am trusting the whole chain: the decisions I
          made during grilling, the spec, bounded tickets, agreed test seams, acceptance criteria,
          the test suite, Playwright, and the implementation skill's code review. I moved most of my
          scrutiny before the code and focused the final human check on the product.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are exceptions. I scan the code for authentication, billing, security-sensitive
          work, migrations, destructive data operations, and changes whose risks the automated
          checks cannot prove. This is a fast, targeted read rather than a detailed review of every
          line.
        </p>
        <p>
          So far, whenever I have manually checked the finished app, the implementation has
          delivered what we agreed. That is a report from my own work, not a promise that the method
          removes defects or fits every team.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="the-tools-can-change">
        <h2 id="the-tools-can-change">The tools can change</h2>
        <p>
          I started using this workflow with Claude Code and later moved to Codex. The analytics
          image comes from Codex, but the workflow is agent-agnostic. It depends on an agent that can
          follow skill files, work inside isolated Git worktrees, run the project's tools, and
          report which checks passed.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also use Cursor's
          <a href="https://www.skills.sh/cursor/plugins/unslop"><code>unslop</code> skill</a> for
          product copy, documentation, and PR text.<sup><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup> It is useful
          when an agent's default explanation is so dense that I cannot understand it. I ask for the
          same meaning in plain language, then make the decision myself.
        </p>
        <p>
          Addy Osmani has a similar
          <a href="https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills">agent-skills collection</a> that covers
          requirements, planning, implementation, testing, review, and shipping.<sup>
            <a href="#ref-5">5</a>
          </sup>
          His collection can select skills automatically based on the task. Matt Pocock's main
          engineering handoffs are deliberately invoked by the human. I have not tried Addy's
          collection yet, but it is close enough to this workflow that I want to try it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="this-is-a-snapshot">
        <h2 id="this-is-a-snapshot">This is a snapshot</h2>
        <p>
          The word "current" in the title is deliberate. Models will change. Skills will change. I
          expect this workflow to change with them.
        </p>
        <p>
          I want to keep spending my attention while the product is still a set of decisions. Once
          those decisions are explicit, each agent gets one ticket with acceptance criteria and a
          fresh context window.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-references" aria-labelledby="references">
        <h2 id="references">References</h2>
        <ol>
          <li id="ref-1">
            Matt Pocock,
            <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills">Engineering skills</a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-2">
            Dex Horthy,
            <a href="https://ai.engineer/talks/context-engineering-for-complex-codebases">
              No Vibes Allowed: Solving Hard Problems in Complex Codebases
            </a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-3">
            OpenAI,
            <a href="https://github.com/openai/plugins/blob/main/plugins/github/skills/yeet/SKILL.md">
              GitHub Yeet skill
            </a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-4">
            Cursor,
            <a href="https://www.skills.sh/cursor/plugins/unslop">Unslop editing skill</a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-5">
            Addy Osmani,
            <a href="https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills">Agent Skills</a>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
